The panel only offered 120 Hz, and it can do 60

WHAT HAPPENS
------------
The surya's panel exposes ONE single mode. It is not the compositor's doing nor
the device tree's: it is nailed down in the driver, which returns a single
`drm_display_mode` with `drm_connector_helper_get_modes_fixed()` -which by
definition registers exactly one-. Measured on the phone:

    $ cat /sys/class/drm/card0/card0-DSI-1/modes
    1080x2400

    $ kscreen-doctor -o
    Modes: 1:1080x2400@120.00*!      <- single mode

So "lowering the hertz" was not a setting that remained to be found: there was no
mode to select.

The driver was generated by `linux-mdss-dsi-panel-driver-generator` from Xiaomi's
vendor device tree, and only the 120 Hz timing was ported.

HOW THIS PANEL CHANGES FPS: BY LENGTHENING THE PORCH, NOT BY LOWERING THE CLOCK
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This is what has to be understood, and what cost one failed attempt. The vendor
device tree (MiCode/Xiaomi_Kernel_OpenSource, surya-q-oss branch,
arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/dsi-panel-nt36672c-tianma-fhd-video.dtsi) says:

    qcom,mdss-dsi-pan-enable-dynamic-fps;
    qcom,mdss-dsi-pan-fps-update = "dfps_immediate_porch_mode_vfp";
    qcom,dsi-supported-dfps-list = <120 90 60 50 30>;

`dfps_immediate_porch_mode_vfp`: the refresh rate is changed by moving the
VERTICAL FRONT PORCH, with the pixel clock CONSTANT. The panel always expects the
same bit clock; what varies is how much blanking there is between frames.

That is why the two modes here share clock, horizontal timing and DSI bit clock,
and only the vertical porch changes:

    120 Hz   vfp   32   vtotal 2464   1205 * 2464 * 120 / 1000 = 356294 kHz
     60 Hz   vfp 2496   vtotal 4928   1205 * 4928 *  60 / 1000 = 356294 kHz

Identical. Moreover the initialisation sequence is the SAME for every fps -the
device tree only carries one `qcom,mdss-dsi-on-command`-, so no new DSI command
is needed.

THE FAILED ATTEMPT, WHICH DESERVES TO BE WRITTEN DOWN
-----------------------------------------------------
The first version of this patch did the obvious thing: same porches and the clock
halved (178147 kHz). The panel fills with GLITCHES.

And it does so silently, which is the dangerous part: EVERYTHING machine-readable
said it was working.

    kscreen-doctor      1080x2400@60.00, mode applied
    wayland-info        refresh: 60.000 Hz
    dmesg               not one dpu, dsi, pll or drm error

It was accepted on that basis, and it was only found out when a person looked at
the panel. The registers are not the screen.

Two hypotheses were tested and both were false:

  1. "The DSC PPS is sent only once in prepare() and is not resent."
     False: the PPS depends on hdisplay and on the slice geometry, not on the
     clock, so resending it would have changed nothing.

  2. "The DSI PLL is not reprogrammed on a hot modeset."
     True that it was not being reprogrammed -measured: the clocks stayed at the
     120 Hz values while the DPU generated 60 vblanks/s-, but that was NOT the
     cause. Forcing a panel cycle brought all three clocks down to exactly half...
     and the glitches stayed the same.

The cause was the one above: you cannot change the panel's clock. Period.

VERIFIED
--------
With the long porch the image comes out CLEAN. Verified on the phone, and this
time looking at the screen, which is what was missing on both previous occasions:

    mode      1080x2400@60.00 applied
    bit_clk   860427759   <- THE SAME as at 120 Hz: the sign that the porch
    pclk      143404627      method is being used and not the clock one
    dmesg     not one dpu/dsi/drm error
    screen    no glitches

Boot too: 120 Hz goes first and carries `DRM_MODE_TYPE_PREFERRED`, so it is the
one used if nobody touches anything. Verified by booting with the installed
kernel (r195).

And even though it comes out clean, the saving will be modest: the screen is LCD
-the device tree wires `backlight = <&pm6150l_wled>`, that is, white LED
backlighting- and the backlight does not depend on the refresh rate. Besides,
with the porch method the DSI clock does not come down either, so the PHY draws
the same. What is saved is that the DPU and the GPU compose 60 frames instead of
120.

